diff --git a/docs/usb-redirection.md b/docs/usb-redirection.md
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..e0d786c
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/usb-redirection.md
@@ -0,0 +1,137 @@
+# USB Redirection over RDP
+
+RDP clients, such as the Windows Remote Desktop client, can redirect USB
+devices to the server over the `URBDRC` dynamic virtual channel
+([MS-RDPEUSB]). GNOME Remote Desktop doesn't handle USB traffic itself.
+Making a redirected device appear on the host needs privileges and kernel
+interfaces that don't belong in a desktop session's service. Instead, it lends
+the session's `URBDRC` channels to a separate program, a USB redirection
+broker, over a unix socket. The broker implements MS-RDPEUSB and attaches the
+devices, for example through USB/IP's `vhci_hcd`.
+
+GNOME Remote Desktop knows nothing about USB. It opens and closes channels when
+the broker asks, and relays their messages.
+
+[MS-RDPEUSB]: https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/openspecs/windows_protocols/ms-rdpeusb/
+
+---
+
+## Configuration
+
+Set the path to the broker's socket with `grdctl`. For a remote assistance,
+remote desktop or headless session:
+
+```
+grdctl rdp set-usb-redirection-socket /run/example-broker/channel.sock
+```
+
+For remote login, add `--system`:
+
+```
+grdctl --system rdp set-usb-redirection-socket /run/example-broker/channel.sock
+```
+
+This sets `usb-redirection-socket` in the `org.gnome.desktop.remote-desktop.rdp`
+schema, or in the system daemon's `grd.conf`. An empty path, which is the default, turns USB redirection
+off. When the path is set but nothing is listening, sessions carry on without
+USB redirection.
+
+The user running the session's `gnome-remote-desktop` must be allowed to
+connect to the socket.
+
+### Remote login
+
+With remote login, the system daemon passes the socket path to the
+`gnome-remote-desktop` instance that runs each session: first the one on the
+login screen, then the one in the user's session after they log in. Each of
+them connects to the broker, so a redirected device is available on the login
+screen. Because a device attached through the broker is visible to the whole
+machine, PAM modules can use it to log in, for example a security key with
+`pam_u2f`, or a fingerprint reader with `fprintd`.
+
+When the user logs in, the client is handed over to the user's session, and
+the broker sees the login screen's connection close and a new one open. The
+client offers its devices again on the new connection, so they are detached and
+attached again.
+
+On the login screen, `gnome-remote-desktop` runs as the login screen's user.
+Recent versions of GDM create those users as needed (`gdm-greeter`,
+`gdm-greeter-2`, …), all with the primary group `gdm`, so a broker that should
+serve the login screen has to let that group connect.
+
+On the login screen, the remote side has only passed the system daemon's RDP
+credentials, not logged in as a user. Attaching USB devices there exposes the
+host's USB drivers to that client. A broker that should only serve logged-in
+users can refuse connections from the login screen's user, which it can tell
+from the socket peer's credentials (`SO_PEERCRED`).
+
+---
+
+## The socket protocol
+
+### Connecting
+
+- GNOME Remote Desktop connects once per RDP session, when the session's
+  dynamic channels are usable. One connection is one session.
+- Either side ends the session by closing the connection. The broker should
+  then detach all of that session's devices.
+
+### Frames
+
+Every message in both directions is a frame: a 12-byte header and then the
+payload.
+
+| Offset | Size | Field |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| 0 | 4 | type |
+| 4 | 4 | channel |
+| 8 | 4 | payload length, at most 16 MiB |
+| 12 | length | payload |
+
+The header fields are `uint32_t` in the host's native byte order, since both
+ends are on the same machine.
+
+`channel` is a number chosen by the broker. It names one instance of the
+`URBDRC` channel within the session, and isn't the DVC channel id on the wire.
+
+| Type | Name | Direction | Payload | Meaning |
+| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
+| 1 | `OPEN` | broker → server | empty | Open a new instance of the `URBDRC` dynamic channel and call it `channel`. |
+| 2 | `OPENED` | server → broker | `uint32_t` DVC channel id | The client accepted the channel; data can flow. |
+| 3 | `DATA` | both | one channel message | One complete DVC message, reassembled from or to be split into DVC PDUs. |
+| 4 | `CLOSE` | broker → server | empty | Close the channel. |
+| 5 | `CLOSED` | server → broker | empty | The channel is closed, or couldn't be opened. |
+
+### What GNOME Remote Desktop does
+
+- **On `OPEN`:** it opens a dynamic channel named `URBDRC`.
+  - If the client accepts it, it sends `OPENED`.
+  - If the client refuses it, or doesn't answer within 10 seconds, it sends
+    `CLOSED`.
+  - If the number is already in use, or 64 channels are already open in the
+    session, it sends `CLOSED`.
+- **On data from the client on a channel:** it sends one `DATA` frame per
+  message.
+- **On `DATA` from the broker:** it writes the payload to that channel as one
+  message. It drops the data if the channel isn't open.
+- **On `CLOSE`:** it closes the channel if it's open, and always answers with
+  `CLOSED`.
+- **When the client closes a channel:** it sends `CLOSED`.
+- **On an unknown frame type or an oversized frame:** it closes the connection.
+
+### A session
+
+1. The broker sends `OPEN` for channel 1, the control channel. If the client
+   has no USB redirection, the answer is `CLOSED`.
+2. On `OPENED`, the broker runs MS-RDPEUSB's capability exchange over
+   channel 1.
+3. For each device the client offers, the broker sends `OPEN` for a new
+   channel number, then talks to that device over it. When the device goes, it
+   sends `CLOSE`, or GNOME Remote Desktop reports `CLOSED`.
+
+---
+
+## Brokers
+
+- [rdp-usb-gw](https://github.com/wrouesnel/rdp-usb-gw) implements the server
+  side of MS-RDPEUSB and attaches devices through USB/IP's `vhci_hcd`.
diff --git a/src/grd-daemon-handover.c b/src/grd-daemon-handover.c
index c8f8751..d2e4e33 100644
--- a/src/grd-daemon-handover.c
+++ b/src/grd-daemon-handover.c
@@ -235,6 +235,8 @@ on_start_handover_finished (GObject      *object,
   g_object_set (G_OBJECT (settings),
                 "rdp-server-cert", certificate,
                 "rdp-server-key", key,
+                "rdp-usb-redirection-socket",
+                grd_dbus_remote_desktop_rdp_handover_get_usb_redirection_socket (proxy),
                 NULL);
 }
 
diff --git a/src/grd-daemon-system.c b/src/grd-daemon-system.c
index 008dda8..dbc9757 100644
--- a/src/grd-daemon-system.c
+++ b/src/grd-daemon-system.c
@@ -458,6 +458,10 @@ static HandoverInterface *
 handover_iface_new (const char      *session_id,
                     GrdRemoteClient *remote_client)
 {
+  GrdDaemon *daemon = GRD_DAEMON (remote_client->daemon_system);
+  GrdContext *context = grd_daemon_get_context (daemon);
+  GrdSettings *settings = grd_context_get_settings (context);
+  g_autofree char *usb_redirection_socket = NULL;
   HandoverInterface *handover;
 
   handover = g_new0 (HandoverInterface, 1);
@@ -478,6 +482,13 @@ handover_iface_new (const char      *session_id,
   g_signal_connect (handover->interface, "handle-get-system-credentials",
                     G_CALLBACK (on_handle_get_system_credentials), remote_client);
 
+  g_object_get (G_OBJECT (settings),
+                "rdp-usb-redirection-socket", &usb_redirection_socket,
+                NULL);
+  grd_dbus_remote_desktop_rdp_handover_set_usb_redirection_socket (
+    handover->interface,
+    usb_redirection_socket ? usb_redirection_socket : "");
+
   return handover;
 }
 
diff --git a/src/grd-settings-system.c b/src/grd-settings-system.c
index 531732a..bf10e11 100644
--- a/src/grd-settings-system.c
+++ b/src/grd-settings-system.c
@@ -371,6 +371,22 @@ write_string (GrdSettingsSystem *settings_system,
                          value);
 }
 
+static void
+read_string (GrdSettingsSystem *settings_system,
+             GKeyFile          *key_file,
+             const char        *group,
+             const char        *key,
+             const char        *settings_name)
+{
+  g_autofree char *value = NULL;
+
+  value = g_key_file_get_string (key_file, group, key, NULL);
+  if (!value)
+    return;
+
+  g_object_set (G_OBJECT (settings_system), settings_name, value, NULL);
+}
+
 static void
 read_filename (GrdSettingsSystem *settings_system,
                GKeyFile          *key_file,
@@ -475,6 +491,7 @@ static const FileSetting rdp_file_settings[] =
   { "tls-cert", "rdp-server-cert-path", read_filename, write_string },
   { "tls-key", "rdp-server-key-path", read_filename, write_string },
   { "port", "rdp-port", read_int, write_int },
+  { "usb-redirection-socket", "rdp-usb-redirection-socket", read_string, write_string },
 };
 
 static void
